00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.250
So next, I'd like to cover
one of our main products

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.730
in this space, And
that's Pipelines API.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:25.010
It's also referred to
as Genomics API, which

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:28.580
was an earlier name of the
product due to the usage being

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.970
almost exclusively
for genomics to start.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:36.560
The product now is available for
any sort of research workload,

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.000
and it works well
for genomic pipelines

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.770
but also can handle
other things as well.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.530
So even though we
can do other things,

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:49.430
one of the more common uses of
it is to do secondary analysis.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.220
And in this case,
we can use it to run

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:56.230
standard tools such as GATK
or other optimized machine

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:59.550
learning tools such
as Deep Variant.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:04.560
By using Pipelines API, we
can optimize for time and cost

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.720
through a number
of different ways.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.020
These include things
like parallel execution,

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.460
being able to use GPUs or
TPUs, or Tensor Processing

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.780
Units, persistent disk
and different disk types,

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.590
as well as different instances
such as preemptable instances,

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.640
which are instances
that are much cheaper

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.880
but may get shut down if
there's a spike in demand.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:31.660
We also work with a
number of workflow engines

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.307
to incorporate directly
with Pipelines API,

00:05:34.307 --> 00:05:35.890
and you can see some
of those partners

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.500
here, and then
also some partners

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:42.190
who provide their own solutions
in this space, such as Fire

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:47.920
Cloud, Ntera, DNA Stack,
Seven Bridges, BC Platforms,

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.000
and a number of others.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.270
The work that we're
doing in this space

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.703
is informed by the interest
that we see in the industry.

00:05:55.703 --> 00:05:57.370
So if there are things
that you're using

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:00.520
or partners you're working
with that aren't available,

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:01.160
reach out.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.110
We'd love to hear
some feedback on that

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.035
so we know what
we can do to help

00:06:05.035 --> 00:06:06.160
drive the industry forward.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:12.320
So some of the workflow
engines that we integrate with

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.490
include Cromwell, dsub,
Nextflow is a newer one

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.830
that we now incorporate
with, and then we're

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.680
working with a number
of others as well

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:25.010
to bring that support to
the other workflow engines.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:28.760
Now, the advantage of working
with a workflow engine

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:33.140
is it abstracts away the process
of you having to start VMs,

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.540
manage the whole
process of that,

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.960
and what you provide
is the container that

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.390
has the task you want to run.

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:44.540
You include the VM, and so any
of the specifications you have

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:48.320
for that VM, ranging from a
very high level definition

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.692
where you might just say you
want a standard 1 instance,

00:06:51.692 --> 00:06:53.150
all the way down
to the point where

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:57.267
you can configure the number
of CPUs, which CPU architecture

00:06:57.267 --> 00:06:59.600
you need if you're taking
advantage of some of the newer

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.490
Intel chips, the ability
to attach GPUs and TPUs

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.580
and specify which ones you
want, and different disk

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:10.610
types as well, which
could include SSDs, even

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:15.360
locally-attached SSDs if you
really need that throughput.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.600
So when you're using
the workflow engine now,

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.330
it's easier for you to run
these in various environments

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.050
and not have to
change everything just

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.700
to move to Google Cloud.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:27.250
You don't have to learn
the intricacies of the API

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.500
because we've done that work
with the workflow engines

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:30.650
themselves.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.790
So you just define
the task you have,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.860
and the workflow engine will
make those calls for you.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.390
Another benefit of
this is that you

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.820
don't have to monitor the
status of all of the tasks

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.910
as they go through,
and the workflow engine

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:47.000
will track when an
operation starts,

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.160
what needs to come next,
when it should start one,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.790
and if it needs to retry a
certain task due to failure

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:53.966
or preemption.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:01.140
So when we use Pipelines API,
it starts these operations using

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.390
a container for each task.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.810
Again, you define the resources
that you need for each task,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.580
and this is something
that you can specify

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:10.500
at an individual
task level instead

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.660
of just the entire pipeline.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.510
So you can really start
to optimize for each task,

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:18.630
and you can realize which parts
need high throughput, which

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.550
parts need memory,
and maybe only

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:25.170
a certain part that needs an
attach accelerator like a GPU,

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.760
or certain parts
of it that may be

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.382
more suitable to being preempted
if you can restart them.

00:08:31.382 --> 00:08:32.840
So we'll take a
look here at a demo

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.320
of using Cromwell to
run the Germline SNPs

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.392
and INDELs from
GATK Best Practices,

00:08:39.392 --> 00:08:40.850
and the demo we're
going to look at

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.820
is actually two
separate pipelines.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.700
And so what we have
here is Cromwell

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.640
in a container and
the first pipeline,

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.820
we send the container
that includes Cromwell,

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:55.520
and the inputs that
we provide to that

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.730
are the WDL, the
workflow definition,

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:00.530
and then a list of
the inputs and options

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:02.330
