been started that's
actually running that job.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.690
And then going to Storage, we
can go ahead and see the output

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:56.097
from that as well as some of
the logs such as standard out

00:12:56.097 --> 00:12:58.180
and error if we need to
troubleshoot some of that.

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:07.260
Next up is Deep Variant.

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:10.558
So Deep Variant is a SNPs
and INDEL variant color

00:13:10.558 --> 00:13:11.850
that uses deep neural networks.

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:17.310
And this is developed
as a collaboration

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:21.270
between Google Research
and Verily Life Sciences.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:23.370
And the Cloud Health Care
and Life Sciences Team

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:26.430
partnered with them to
optimize this for cost

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.280
and speed running on GCP.

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:30.780
And if you look at
the documentation

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:34.597
pages for Deep Variant in
our Cloud documentation,

00:13:34.597 --> 00:13:36.180
there's a couple of
different settings

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:39.180
we provide there of a version
that's optimized for time

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:41.670
and a version that's
optimized for cost.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.670
And we can do this by using
different resources, attaching

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:46.362
GPUs, for example.

00:13:46.362 --> 00:13:48.570
And by changing that, we
can find the right trade-off

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.790
between cost and time that
works for your business

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.232
or your research.

00:13:53.232 --> 00:13:54.690
So in this case,
we're going to run

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:56.850
Deep Variant from a
Docker container--

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:00.425
very similar to the last ones.

00:14:00.425 --> 00:14:02.050
So we'll take a look
at the script here

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:04.060
that's going to
start Deep Variant.

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.890
Again, we set a
number of variables

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:08.920
at the beginning, such as
our project, the input,

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.560
the reference genome
that we want to use,

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:13.708
the zone where we're
going to have this run.

00:14:13.708 --> 00:14:15.250
And then at the
bottom is the command

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:18.250
that's making the
call to Pipelines API,

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:22.070
and that will look very similar
to the last ones as well.

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:25.170
So once we start it, we
get that operation ID.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:27.470
In this one, we'll
jump ahead here,

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:29.980
and we can go ahead and look
at the output in our storage

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:32.440
bucket and get to
that VCF file as well.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:39.510
One of the newest additions
we have here is Nextflow.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:42.330
We recently added this
a couple of months ago.

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:45.540
If you're not familiar, Nextflow
is a multi-platform workflow

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:48.570
engine that supports a
variety of back ends,

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:53.160
ranging from various Cloud
providers to on prem,

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:57.000
to HPC clusters, to
even running locally.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.610
And what we built in here in
working with the community

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.860
to develop this is
the ability for you

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:05.220
to choose Google
Pipelines and Google Cloud

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:10.750
Platform as the back end where
you want to run those tasks.

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:12.870
So this allows you to
take an existing workflow

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:16.040
that you have that may be
running somewhere else,

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:19.350
and you can now move that
to run with Pipelines API

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:21.420
by changing the
executor and then

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.740
a couple of the settings
in there as well--

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:27.550
again, things like
project machine types.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.910
And then, in this case,
it will use Pipelines API

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:32.910
as the back end for that, and so
all of your computation, then,

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:37.730
will run through Pipelines
on Google Compute Engine.

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:40.010
There's also an
option with Nextflow

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:42.350
where Nextflow can set
up a cluster directly

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:45.990
on GCE with configurable
master and worker nodes,

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:48.615
so that's an option
you can use as well.

00:15:48.615 --> 00:15:50.240
The example we're
going to look at here

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:54.020
is with running an RNAseq
proof of concept that's using

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:56.900
Pipelines API as the back end.

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:00.020
Again, we're going to do this
whole thing in the Cloud Shell.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:02.120
So we don't actually have
to install the Nextflow

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:04.760
application on our local
machine, although you can

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:05.840
if you'd like.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:08.747
But we'll show a demo here
of doing it all in the Cloud.

00:16:08.747 --> 00:16:11.330
And then we're going to use that
installed version of Nextflow

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:16.290
that's in Cloud Shell to go
ahead and run our pipeline.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:17.780
So let's take a
look at the config

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:20.570
here, our Nextflow config,
and you'll see at the top

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.848
there where we set
Google Pipelines,

00:16:22.848 --> 00:16:24.890
and this is where you
would change that depending

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:27.380
where you want it to run.

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:29.570
We then set a couple
of specific options,

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:33.243
such as the instance types we
want to use and the project.

00:16:33.243 --> 00:16:35.660
And then we'll go ahead and
start that version of Nextflow

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:38.540
right now running in the Cloud.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:42.230
As the tasks go through, you'll
see an ID on the left there,

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:44.570
and we can use those to
track the resources like we

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:48.560
saw before, going into the
operations and Compute Engine

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:52.162
to actually see which instances
are doing which m tasks

00:16:52.162 --> 00:16:53.120
and follow up on those.

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.380
Next up, we'll get into
a Variant Transforms.

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:03.560
So Variant Transforms is a
tool that we open-sourced

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:07.400
for transforming and processing
VCF files to bring them

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.550
into BigQuery to do analytics.

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:13.970
Now, Variant Transforms
runs using Dataflow,

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:16.339
which if you're not familiar,
supports the Apache Beam

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:20.490
SDK in both batch and streaming
mode, although in this case,

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:23.040
we're going to use it in batch.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.230
And then variant
Transforms also supports

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:27.582
both Direct runner
and Dataflow runner,

00:17:27.582 --> 00:17:29.040
and you can pick
which one of those

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:31.680
is ideal for the
operation you're running.

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:39.090
So some of the challenges that
we face in tertiary analysis

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:42.150
include trying to analyze
large amounts of data

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:44.382
and join that with
public datasets

00:17:44.382 --> 00:17:45.840
or perhaps with
datasets that we're

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:50.220
receiving from a collaborator
or at a different facility.

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.350
The ability to
join those variants

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:54.093
with different
annotation sources

00:17:54.093 --> 00:17:56.010
where we're trying to
understand an annotation

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:58.950
specific to that organism--

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:02.510
we have tasks like QA
and QC that we're doing.

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:06.340
We may have multi-sample
processing as well.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:08.910
And then doing things
like association analysis,

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:12.540
population analysis, GWAS.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.700
So for all of those
things, BigQuery

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.200
is a great tool for that.

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.520
We'll get into that
in a second here.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.370
But to be able to
support that, we really

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:23.250
need a way to import
this data into BigQuery

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:26.820
in a reliable, reproducible
way with a standard format.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:30.650
And Variant Transforms
is what is that for us.

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:33.560
So Variant Transforms uses
parallel batch processing

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:37.940
and Dataflow to do this,
reading in VCF files,

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:42.410
either single files,
multiple files, and then

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:46.100
we'll end up dumping
those into BigQuery table.

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.140
Now, in addition
to importing, we

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:50.600
realize that sometimes
you may have a tool that

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:53.150
still needs VCF files.

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:55.590
The reality is a lot of things
have already been written,

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:58.130
and they may not have an
integration with BigQuery,

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:00.890
and so you may need
the VCF files back.

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:03.410
Variant Transforms also
supports pulling things back

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:06.620
out of BigQuery to
give you a VCF file.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:09.380
So you can maybe
identify a subgroup

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:13.220
from your data that's in
BigQuery, export that to a VCF

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:15.440
that you can then put
into your other tools,

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.780
and really use BigQuery
as that storage as well.

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:22.580
Another recent addition
to Variant Transforms

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:25.280
is support for
doing annotations,

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:30.170
and right now, we have support
for doing VEP annotation,

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:32.630
either importing something
that's been annotated

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:36.717
or performing annotation on a
dataset that you already have.

00:19:36.717 --> 00:19:38.300
As we continue this
development, we're

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:40.350
looking at other
annotation sources as well.

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:42.350
So if there are others
that you're interested in

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:43.940
or something that
would be useful,

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.065
again, we'd love to hear
the feedback there and see

00:19:46.065 --> 00:19:47.718
what would be useful for you.

00:19:47.718 --> 00:19:49.760
At the end of the talk,
we've got a mailing list,

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:51.343
and I'll point that
out, but if you've

00:19:51.343 --> 00:19:54.298
got feedback or questions, feel
free to send it to that group,

00:19:54.298 --> 00:19:55.590
and we'd love to hear from you.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:01.410
So this example here,
looking at this screenshot,

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.560
is running Variant
Transforms on the 2,500

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.170
samples of 1,000 Genomes.

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:10.710
And this particular set is
about 81 million variants,

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:14.340
so it ends up being an
81 million row table.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:15.900
And the screenshot
we're looking at

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:21.000
is the Dataflow console
in Google Cloud console.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:23.490
And you can see
here-- this task is

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:27.300
about 100 days of compute time,
and we've completed it here

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.940
in just under two hours.

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:31.500
But you'll notice
we could go faster.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.110
If we look at the right,
that blue line on the chart

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:36.280
where that kind of plateaus--

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:37.890
so that line is the
number of workers

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:40.410
that we had working
on the task, and you

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:42.360
can see it hit a
limit there, which was

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:44.830
the quota that I've assigned.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:48.630
And in this case, I have had
a quota set for disk space,

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:51.990
for how much SSD I was willing
to use at the same time.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:54.570
So you can use things like these
quotas to help control costs

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:58.260
or figure out how much you're
willing to do at a single time.

00:20:58.260 --> 00:21:01.170
In this particular case, it
scaled up to about 400, workers

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:03.180
and then that's
where it stopped.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:05.670
If you wanted to increase
that, we can increase the time.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:07.390
It will be done faster.

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:09.900
And again, that's where you
get into the balance of cost

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:11.730
versus time.

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.070
Now, in running it
with more disk space,

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:16.630
you're obviously going to
pay for more disk space,

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:18.513
but you'll have much
less compute time.

00:21:18.513 --> 00:21:21.180
And so that's where you start to
balance the differences in cost

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.950
of compute versus
cost of storage.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:26.630
So the next sample
we'll take a look at

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:29.390
is basically running this
on a smaller subset of data

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:31.130
that's about 100 variants.

00:21:31.130 --> 00:21:32.330
We use that just for scale.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:36.740
So when we take a
look at the script

00:21:36.740 --> 00:21:39.410
we're going to use to
run Variant Transforms,

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:42.140
we list things like the
input bucket with our VCF,

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:45.420
the output table in
BigQuery, a temp location,

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:47.930
and then some information
about our product.

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:51.740
At the bottom, you'll also see
a Google Pipelines command.

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:53.720
We're actually running
Variant Transforms

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:57.480
through Google Pipelines,
just like we did before.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:00.600
So once that starts,
we get that operation,

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.440
we can come to Compute Engine,
and we can see the main node

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:04.970
here that we've
started, and this

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:06.428
is the worker that's
actually going

00:22:06.428 --> 00:22:08.990
to kick off that Dataflow job.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:10.850
So then coming into
Dataflow, we can

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:13.190
start to get into the
specifics of that run.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:15.560
We can follow it to see
where it's at in the process,

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:18.140
understand the
resource consumption.

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:20.000
And once it's done,
we'll go into BigQuery

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:22.360
to see the output.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:24.020
Coming into the
dataset and table,

00:22:24.020 --> 00:22:26.360
you'll see the
schema listed here.

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:28.360
And this is the schema
that we use for variants,

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:30.580
and you can see the information
as well as the types,

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:33.670
for example, so you know
how to query that field.

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:35.680
We can look at some
details about that,

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:39.130
showing us the size of the
table, the rows, the location

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:41.200
where it's stored.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:42.700
And then this
preview field lets us

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:44.680
see a sample set of the data.

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:46.910
So it shows us how
the data is stored.

00:22:46.910 --> 00:22:48.560
And if you're more
of a visual learner,

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:49.840
this is a spot you
can come to see

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:52.298
what that looks like before
you start writing your queries.

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:59.910
So once it's there, BigQuery is
our serverless, scalable data

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:02.150
warehouse.

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:06.410
And BigQuery scales to
petabytes of storage.

00:23:06.410 --> 00:23:09.440
It provides a standard SQL
dialect for doing queries,

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:13.110
so you don't have to learn
a new language to use it.

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:16.730
One of the features is that
it actually separates compute

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:19.032
and storage from each other.

00:23:19.032 --> 00:23:20.990
And so in this case,
particularly in a research

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:23.510
environment, it
is very important

00:23:23.510 --> 00:23:26.030
because as a data host, you
know exactly what you're

00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:28.052
going to be on the hook for.

00:23:28.052 --> 00:23:29.510
You upload a data
set, you're going

00:23:29.510 --> 00:23:31.760
to pay the storage cost
of that, and you're only

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:33.560
going to pay the storage cost.

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:36.200
You're not worried about what
analytics someone else is

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:38.930
going to run against that.

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:41.540
And as a user, you know that
you're paying for an analysis

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:44.720
that you're doing,
and we show you

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:48.680
how much you can expect
to pay for those as well.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:51.920
As a data host, we also
offer a lot of different ways

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:55.382
to do access control, and you
can do some fine-grained access

00:23:55.382 --> 00:23:56.840
control as well if
you're providing

00:23:56.840 --> 00:24:00.420
your data set to different
collaborators, for example.

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:02.030
One of the ways
that we do that is

00:24:02.030 --> 00:24:05.690
through using authorized
views, and authorized views

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:09.500
are a way to let you share your
query results without actually

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.790
sharing the underlying data.

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:15.110
So let's say you have a
table with some dataset,

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:17.630
and you want to give
access to a subset of that,

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:19.850
whether that's certain
columns that aren't relevant

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:22.760
for that person, maybe
its certain rows,

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:25.010
or due to different consent
or usage agreements,

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:27.290
you're only allowed to
provide certain samples

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:29.240
to a particular user.

00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:32.700
In that case, you write a query
that pulls out that subgroup,

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:36.570
and you save your results
as an authorized view.

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:39.780
The end user that you now give
access to the authorized view

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:42.157
queries that view as
though it were a table.

00:24:42.157 --> 00:24:43.740
So they don't know
anything different.

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:44.995
They think that's the table.

00:24:44.995 --> 00:24:46.620
They use the same
syntax and everything

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:48.448
as though they were
querying the table,

00:24:48.448 --> 00:24:50.490
but they don't get access
to the underlying data,

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:54.100
and you decide what
they get to see.

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:57.400
Some other features in
BigQuery are query validation.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:24:59.982
So on the right here, you
can see this green checkbox,

00:24:59.982 --> 00:25:01.690
and that's going to
let us know that it's

00:25:01.690 --> 00:25:03.670
a valid query we wrote.

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:05.908
If it's not valid, we
get a little red x.

00:25:05.908 --> 00:25:07.450
If you hover over
that, it will start

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:09.680
to give you some
feedback as well

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:11.680
and let you know what's
missing in the syntax,

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:16.017
give you some tips as to
how to maybe fix that.

00:25:16.017 --> 00:25:17.600
And then, next to
that, the green text

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:19.850
as well tells you
how much data it's

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:23.190
expected to process
when you run this query.

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:26.150
So the pricing
model for BigQuery

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:28.710
is storage and data processing.

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:30.770
So time is not something
that you're charged on,

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:33.980
but you're charged on the amount
of data that gets processed.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:35.640
And so we show you
here how much data

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:38.810
we expect to process if
you were to run this query,

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.060
and it lets you understand,
is this really a query you

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:41.833
want to run?

00:25:41.833 --> 00:25:43.250
Or is it something
where maybe you

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:47.810
need to optimize your query
before you actually run that?

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:49.460
We provide a number
of different ways

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:52.280
to actually integrate
with BigQuery.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:53.840
We have the UI
here, so if that's

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:55.340
something you're
more familiar with,

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:57.440
you can run your
queries from here.

00:25:57.440 --> 00:25:59.570
You don't have to leave
your browser window open

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:00.860
when you run them.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:02.780
Your queries will
get started as a job.

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:05.630
You can come back
and find them later.

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.580
If you run the same
query again, results

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:09.783
are cached for an amount
of time as well so

00:26:09.783 --> 00:26:11.450
that if you were
running the same thing,

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:15.540
you're not necessarily getting
charged every time you do that.

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:19.090
We also provide a command line
tool for running your queries.

00:26:19.090 --> 00:26:20.640
And so in that case,
that's something

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:22.290
you can run on
your local machine

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:24.797
through the Google
Cloud SDK, or you

00:26:24.797 --> 00:26:26.880
can do it through the Cloud
console, which already

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:29.790
has that tool installed.

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:32.220
There's also an API
for it, as well as

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:34.320
a number of client libraries.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:39.240
The client libraries we include
right now our Java, Go, Python,

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:42.647
Ruby, Node.js, and C#.

00:26:42.647 --> 00:26:44.730
The advantages of this
mean that if you've already

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:47.670
got an application in
one of those languages,

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:49.590
you don't have to write
a separate integration

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:50.850
for BigQuery.

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.760
You can import the BigQuery
library for your language,

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:55.710
have your query
happen right there,

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:57.702
and get your results
in the query.

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:04.150
So when we're using
BigQuery as a variant store,

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:06.037
one of the things we
need to standardize here

00:27:06.037 --> 00:27:07.620
is the schema that
we're going to use.

00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:13.750
And this schema
here that we have

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:17.520
is based on standard
VCF, and the way

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:21.160
we represent this through
a few different data types,

00:27:21.160 --> 00:27:24.960
so we have simple fields
such as integers and strings.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:29.920
We have nested fields which show
up in here listed as a record.

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:32.840
And then, we also can have
repeated fields as well.

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:35.890
So in this case, we may have
multiple alternate bases,

00:27:35.890 --> 00:27:37.517
and within alternate
bases, there

00:27:37.517 --> 00:27:39.350
are different ones we
want to keep track of.

00:27:39.350 --> 00:27:43.970
So we represent those as
a repeated nested field.

00:27:43.970 --> 00:27:46.990
We also store the information
from the VCF header--

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.960
so things that are in format
and info, for example.

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:52.282
You can see at the bottom
here under alternate bases,

00:27:52.282 --> 00:27:54.490
those are things that we've
pulled out of the header,

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:56.470
and then we'll go
ahead and store those.

00:27:56.470 --> 00:28:00.060
Again, we can also put
those back into the VCF

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:04.060
if you need to pull that
back out of BigQuery.

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:06.730
This is suitable for storing
single sample or multiple

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:11.950
samples, and Variant Transforms,
when you're importing,

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:16.760
is also able to validate a
VCF before you import it.

00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:19.390
So for example,
if you have a lot

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:22.450
of samples you're importing at
once, the last thing you want

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:24.220
is to get all the way
to the end and then

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:26.860
have it fail because you have
some malformed records in one

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:28.460
of your files.

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:30.520
So we can actually do
a quick validation step

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:32.062
where it'll go
through before and let

00:28:32.062 --> 00:28:34.425
you know if there's any
issues, and you can decide

00:28:34.425 --> 00:28:35.800
what you want to
do at that point

00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:37.690
if it finds a malformed record.

00:28:37.690 --> 00:28:39.730
Whether you want to try
to guess what to do,

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:42.643
if you want to ignore it, if
you want to abort the task--

00:28:42.643 --> 00:28:44.560
that's something that
you can configure there.

00:28:48.020 --> 00:28:50.690
And then again, as I mentioned,
the ability to export that

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:52.550
back to VCF as well.

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:54.260
Because we have this
standard schema,

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:57.660
we can get it back
to that same spot.

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:01.198
If you're querying multiple
independently-repeated fields,

00:29:01.198 --> 00:29:02.990
in that case, you're
going to actually need

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:04.530
to unnest those fields.

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:06.320
And so we'll flatten
either the table

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:08.450
or flatten part of
the table, and we've

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:11.160
got some documentation
on our page

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:13.040
as well about how
to flatten that,

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:15.860
whether you're trying to
flatten an entire table or just

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:16.850
specific fields.

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:21.290
So the following
slides, we're going

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:24.560
to go through a few examples
of running some queries,

00:29:24.560 --> 00:29:29.160
and the sample set here is
just over 10,000 samples,

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:32.920
which is about 361 million
variants that we're using.

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:37.500
And the initial import of
that is a 23-terabyte table.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.530
Now, these were imported
using Variant Transforms,

00:29:40.530 --> 00:29:42.510
and I actually did
this in four batches,

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:45.300
and that snapshot we saw
earlier from Dataflow

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:48.750
was one of the batches where I
was actually adding 2,500 more

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:52.680
samples to this existing table.

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.420
Now, when you're
doing those imports,

00:29:54.420 --> 00:29:56.400
again, you decide if
you want a new table

00:29:56.400 --> 00:29:59.250
or if you want to append
it to an existing one.

00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:01.600
And when you append it
to an existing table,

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:03.370
it's an atomic operation.

00:30:03.370 --> 00:30:05.850
So nothing is actually
there until the whole job's

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:06.840
completed.

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:09.090
So you don't have to worry
about it failing at the end

00:30:09.090 --> 00:30:11.550
and ending up with a
mismatch of records,

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:13.590
or if someone's running
a query while you're

00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:16.593
updating the records, they'll
get one version or the other,

00:30:16.593 --> 00:30:18.010
depending when
they run the query,

00:30:18.010 --> 00:30:21.780
but you're never going
to get a mix of both.

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:23.780
So we're going to go
through the next few slides

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:26.420
and look at some tips
for optimizing queries,

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:27.920
and the things are
going to optimize

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:34.010
for are runtime storage and the
amount of data that we process.

00:30:34.010 --> 00:30:36.020
Now, as I mentioned,
the costs of BigQuery

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:37.880
are related to the
amount of data you

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:40.520
process and the
storage, so those

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:43.412
are easy things we
would think to optimize

00:30:43.412 --> 00:30:44.870
because they
drastically can reduce

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:46.940
the cost that we pay for this.

00:30:46.940 --> 00:30:49.250
In addition, runtime
can also be important

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:52.610
depending what you're using
the output of this query for.

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:55.190
If you have other processes
in your business or something

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:57.770
else in your lab that
depends on this output,

00:30:57.770 --> 00:31:00.200
there might be times when
the runtime is actually

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:03.792
more important to you than the
cost, or equally as important.

00:31:03.792 --> 00:31:05.750
So we'll look at how to
optimize there as well.

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:11.890
So this first query where to
look at-- a fairly simple one,

00:31:11.890 --> 00:31:14.260
and we're just counting
all the variants,

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:17.030
and we're going to order
them by chromosome.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.060
And so I do this because
this is a query that

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:22.107
shows where we're actually
going to have to go through,

00:31:22.107 --> 00:31:24.190
and we're going to have
to process all of the rows

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:27.068
because we're going to
count all of the variants.

00:31:27.068 --> 00:31:29.110
So we're going to go
through and count everything

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:32.430
where there is a genotype
greater than zero,

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:35.750
and then we're going to
group those by chromosome.

00:31:35.750 --> 00:31:38.780
And so in here, you'll see
that group by reference name,

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:41.163
and that's where we'll
go ahead and order those.

00:31:41.163 --> 00:31:43.330
And once they're ordered,
we'll actually go through,

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:47.760
and we'll count them in each
group and report them back.

00:31:47.760 --> 00:31:51.310
So when we run this on the
initially imported table,

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:55.010
it processes about
13 terabytes of data,

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:58.220
and it takes a little
over four minutes to run.

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:00.540
That is expensive
and time-consuming.

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:02.242
We can do much better
in both of those,

00:32:02.242 --> 00:32:03.950
and we'll show you a
couple of techniques

00:32:03.950 --> 00:32:05.282
of how you can deal with that.

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:09.850
So the first
optimization technique

00:32:09.850 --> 00:32:13.525
we have is partitioning
and clustering of tables.

00:32:13.525 --> 00:32:14.900
Now, in this case,
at the bottom,

00:32:14.900 --> 00:32:18.010
you'll see that we cluster this
table on reference name, which

00:32:18.010 --> 00:32:21.403
is the chromosome, and then
the start and end position.

00:32:21.403 --> 00:32:23.320
So essentially, we're
taking all the variants,

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:25.550
and we're actually
lining them up in order.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:28.530
And that's how we're going
to physically store the data.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:30.430
So actually,
BigQuery is now going

00:32:30.430 --> 00:32:33.640
to collocate the data
that's in the same cluster

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:35.180
in the same place.

00:32:35.180 --> 00:32:37.330
So if you have certain
queries, they'll

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:39.760
be optimized by knowing
where to go in which data

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:42.070
you have to access.

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:45.340
In this case, it can reduce
scans of unnecessary data.

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:47.700
Or in the case of
this particular query,

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:51.640
the benefit we get is that now
the data is already grouped.

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:54.310
So when we try to go ahead and
order those and group them,

00:32:54.310 --> 00:32:56.090
everything is already
clustered together,

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:59.250
so it's a much
quicker process to do.

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:00.750
Now, you'll notice
here in this one,

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:02.760
the table is still
the same size.

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:05.910
It's still a 23-terabyte
table, but we'll

00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:08.820
expect a speed increase due
to the way that it's stored.

00:33:11.570 --> 00:33:14.100
So in this, just
like before, this one

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:17.360
is going to process
13 terabytes of data.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:21.070
So again, it's still a fairly
expensive query for us,

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:23.320
and we're not filtering
in this query, which

00:33:23.320 --> 00:33:26.270
is why we're not saving any
of the processing there.

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:27.910
But you can see the
time that it took

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:30.620
here is just under 45 seconds.

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:33.940
So it's over five times faster
by having that data together

00:33:33.940 --> 00:33:34.750
in the same place.

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:40.060
The next type of optimization
we're going to do

00:33:40.060 --> 00:33:42.250
is starting to optimize
for the types of queries

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:44.050
that we're running.

00:33:44.050 --> 00:33:46.120
And so in this case, we
take the clustered table

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:49.030
that we have and then
extract the call sets

00:33:49.030 --> 00:33:52.090
for the homozygous reference
calls because we can infer that

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:53.060
from the reference.

00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:55.730
So we don't need to store
a duplicate of that.

00:33:55.730 --> 00:33:57.460
And then for the no
calls, we actually

00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:00.700
pull that out and just store
the no call as a Boolean,

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:03.580
as a true or false
of is it a no call.

00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:05.260
So in that case,
we're now not going

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:08.050
to have to unnest that field
to get in there and see that.

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:09.880
We can check the
Boolean and save

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:11.500
on the amount of
data we're processing

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:14.150
and the amount of
data we're storing.

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:16.179
So this table now is
about three times smaller

00:34:16.179 --> 00:34:19.432
than the last one, coming in
at just over eight terabytes.

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:25.540
And when we run that same query
on this clustered and compacted

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:28.270
table, we don't have to
process the entire call

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:31.870
record to check if there's a
genotype greater than zero.

00:34:31.870 --> 00:34:34.850
We can figure out which records
those are, and in this case,

00:34:34.850 --> 00:34:38.810
we did it by processing less
than a terabyte of data.

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:41.560
So in this case, we
process 18 times less data

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:45.310
than the original, meaning the
cost of this query is 5% or so

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:48.969
of what that original one
was, not to mention the time.

00:34:48.969 --> 00:34:50.469
We look at this
query now, we run it

00:34:50.469 --> 00:34:53.699
in under 10 seconds,
which is 25 times faster

00:34:53.699 --> 00:34:55.449
than that original
table that we imported.

00:34:58.070 --> 00:35:02.370
So let's look at some of the
other advantages of clustering.

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:03.530
So this query now--

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:05.820
we're doing the same
thing we did before.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:09.050
We're doing that variant
count, but we're only

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:11.060
going to check chromosome 1.

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:13.850
And the way we're doing
it is with a WHERE clause.

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:15.470
Now, the WHERE clause
is not actually

00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:17.688
going to change how
much data we touch.

00:35:17.688 --> 00:35:19.730
It's still going to have
to go through and check.

00:35:19.730 --> 00:35:22.130
The WHERE clause is great
at restricting how much data

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:23.630
we get back, but
it doesn't really

00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:27.610
help us with how much data
we process, generally.

00:35:27.610 --> 00:35:29.930
And so if we look at
that when we run it,

00:35:29.930 --> 00:35:33.320
you'll see it still processes
13 terabytes of data.

00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:36.820
Now, we did this in 30 seconds
as opposed to the four minutes

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:39.970
because we didn't actually have
to do the group and the count,

00:35:39.970 --> 00:35:42.070
but we still had to go
through all of that data.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.252
So when we start to use
the clustered table,

00:35:47.252 --> 00:35:48.710
so now where it's
actually ordered,

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:53.110
and BigQuery knows where to go
to find different sets of that,

00:35:53.110 --> 00:35:54.860
we can see on the right
here that this one

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:58.790
says it may process up
to 13 terabytes still.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:01.790
Now, the number we give there
is a worst-case scenario.

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:03.560
We don't know all the
time how much data

00:36:03.560 --> 00:36:05.547
we're going to access
until we actually do it,

00:36:05.547 --> 00:36:07.130
but we can give you
a worst-case guess

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:09.210
of what we may have to touch.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:13.292
So in this case, it says that
it could run up to 13 terabytes.

00:36:13.292 --> 00:36:14.750
But when we actually
run it, you'll

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:17.770
go ahead and see the difference.

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:19.650
So in this case, we
actually only process

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:21.690
one terabyte of data.

00:36:21.690 --> 00:36:24.750
Because BigQuery knows
where chromosome 1 has

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:28.525
been clustered, it only has to
hit that particular section,

00:36:28.525 --> 00:36:30.150
and it knows it can
ignore all the rest

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:32.100
of the data in the
table because everything

00:36:32.100 --> 00:36:34.713
that is collocated together.

00:36:34.713 --> 00:36:36.630
Now, it runs in about
the same amount of time,

00:36:36.630 --> 00:36:39.533
around 30 seconds,
which lets us know

00:36:39.533 --> 00:36:40.950
that the majority
of the time here

00:36:40.950 --> 00:36:45.970
is probably the counting and
not the actual finding of it.

00:36:45.970 --> 00:36:47.910
So the time is the
same, but processing

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:52.980
it is 13 times less data, again
leading to a direct relation

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:54.672
to our cost reduction.

00:36:57.270 --> 00:36:59.762
Now, if we keep optimizing
here, and we use that clustered

00:36:59.762 --> 00:37:01.970
and compacted table-- the
one where we had pulled out

00:37:01.970 --> 00:37:05.600
the reference and the no calls--

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:08.850
remember that originally
this process, 13 terabytes,

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.060
took about 30 seconds.

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:13.250
In this version, we're
now only processing

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:17.750
60 gigabytes of data,
so 200 times less data.

00:37:17.750 --> 00:37:20.780
That's barely a blip on the
price sheet at this point.

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:23.240
And we're doing it
five times faster still

00:37:23.240 --> 00:37:27.290
in coming in at just under six
seconds to do that same count.

00:37:31.630 --> 00:37:34.370
So next, if we look at a
little more complicated query,

00:37:34.370 --> 00:37:37.890
if we're checking the
transition to transversion SNPs,

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:40.500
this is a query that will
require accessing almost all

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:42.265
of the data, so we
expect that we're

00:37:42.265 --> 00:37:44.640
going to actually have to
touch most of our entire table.

00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:51.260
On the originally imported data,
this processed 20 terabytes out

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:53.790
of our original
23-terabyte table

00:37:53.790 --> 00:37:55.985
and came in at just
over eight minutes.

00:37:55.985 --> 00:37:57.860
So we'll look at a couple
of different things

00:37:57.860 --> 00:38:01.490
here of using those same
optimized tables we had before

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:04.160
to see what the impact is
on this particular query.

00:38:06.670 --> 00:38:10.200
So again, this calculation
still requires accessing

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:11.820
the same amount of data.

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:14.070
So on a clustered
table, it didn't save us

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:16.980
anything on our actual
cost of computation.

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:20.220
We still had to touch
about 20 terabytes of data,

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:22.980
but we did cut the time
in half and now ran it

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:24.810
in about three and a
half minutes instead

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:26.633
of the original eight.

00:38:26.633 --> 00:38:28.050
So when we start
to look at what's

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:30.670
important to your business
for a particular task,

00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:34.020
this is an option where even
something like clustering

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:36.850
can still help with some
areas of performance.

00:38:36.850 --> 00:38:38.670
And you can find what
the best optimization

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:40.378
is for the types of
queries you're using.

00:38:43.150 --> 00:38:46.810
When we run this on that
clustered and compact table,

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:49.560
we've now cut the time
to be less than a minute.

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:52.502
And in this case, that process
is about seven terabytes.

00:38:52.502 --> 00:38:54.460
Now, remember, this was
an eight-terabyte table

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:56.043
to begin with, so
we're still touching

00:38:56.043 --> 00:38:58.120
almost the entire table.

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:02.080
But it's still a significantly
lower amount of data

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:03.940
that we're having
to touch, and again,

00:39:03.940 --> 00:39:08.380
about three times faster, coming
in at just under a minute.

00:39:08.380 --> 00:39:10.570
So as we go through
this, we're currently

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:13.420
looking at reviewing some
of the schemas we have

00:39:13.420 --> 00:39:16.240
as well as some of these
optimization techniques

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:19.630
to go ahead and see what we can
do to provide optimized schemas

00:39:19.630 --> 00:39:22.932
or provide best practices
for certain types of queries.

00:39:22.932 --> 00:39:24.390
So again, that's
another area where

00:39:24.390 --> 00:39:26.080
we'd love to solicit feedback.

00:39:26.080 --> 00:39:27.880
If you've got certain
queries that you're

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:29.830
running or things that
aren't working well

00:39:29.830 --> 00:39:31.552
or things you're
looking to optimize,

00:39:31.552 --> 00:39:33.010
we've got a mailing
list that we'll

00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:36.340
discuss at the end that's got a
number of users of the product

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:38.900
as well as a bunch of
members of our team.

00:39:38.900 --> 00:39:40.360
And we can help
try to figure out

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:42.040
what's the best
solution for you and see

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:43.870
if there's something we can
bring back to the community

00:39:43.870 --> 00:39:44.470
there as well.

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:50.740
So one of the other
advantages with BigQuery

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:53.770
is using the client libraries,
such as the Python Client

00:39:53.770 --> 00:39:57.383
Library, to integrate
with Jupyter notebooks.

00:39:57.383 --> 00:39:59.800
So in this, you can connect
your Jupyter notebook directly

00:39:59.800 --> 00:40:02.470
to BigQuery, which
we'll use BigQuery

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:05.080
to do the compute and all
of the heavy lifting of any

00:40:05.080 --> 00:40:07.780
of the filtering and ordering
to give you your data back,

00:40:07.780 --> 00:40:10.390
and you're not depending
on your hosted runtime

00:40:10.390 --> 00:40:13.540
to actually do the
hard computations.

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:14.920
So the way this works--

00:40:14.920 --> 00:40:16.900
you paste your
query as an argument

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:19.810
when you've imported
the BigQuery library.

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:22.390
You run that, you get the
results back in your notebook,

00:40:22.390 --> 00:40:23.890
and if you've got
something in there

00:40:23.890 --> 00:40:26.892
already where you're using
a data frame, for example,

00:40:26.892 --> 00:40:28.600
you can get that data
back from BigQuery,

00:40:28.600 --> 00:40:30.730
put that into the
data frame, and not

00:40:30.730 --> 00:40:32.950
have to change the existing
notebooks you have,

00:40:32.950 --> 00:40:34.812
and keep using
your same analysis,

00:40:34.812 --> 00:40:36.520
just changing the data
source that you're

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:39.370
using on the back end.

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:42.880
Some different options we have
for running notebooks here

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:46.480
include Collaboratory, which
is a product by the research

00:40:46.480 --> 00:40:48.852
team at Google that
they provide for use.

00:40:48.852 --> 00:40:50.310
And this is hosted
on a Google back

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:53.710
end, although there are
limitations to the runtime

00:40:53.710 --> 00:40:57.270
and what you can choose since
it's a hosted environment.

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.680
There is Cloud Datalab,
which is a product

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:03.635
that you run in your own
Google Cloud Platform project

00:41:03.635 --> 00:41:05.260
and allows you to
host a notebook there

00:41:05.260 --> 00:41:07.127
that you want to connect to.

00:41:07.127 --> 00:41:09.460
And then we've got a product
that we announced this week

00:41:09.460 --> 00:41:13.540
as well that went to beta
called AI Platform notebooks,

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:17.480
and this is essentially
Jupyter notebooks as a service.

00:41:17.480 --> 00:41:21.100
And this supports a variety
of different frameworks

00:41:21.100 --> 00:41:25.030
and packages, including
TensorFlow and PyTorch,

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:26.950
as well as a lot of
packages pre-installed

00:41:26.950 --> 00:41:31.060
that people are used to,
like NumPy and whatnot.

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:34.480
Now, AI Platform notebooks
also has a built in integration

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:35.500
with GitHub.

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:39.040
So we can use that for syncing
or backing up our notebooks

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:41.825
and also using that to
share among other people.

00:41:41.825 --> 00:41:44.200
So if you need to share your
notebooks with collaborators

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:47.710
either in your lab, in
your company, with others,

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:49.420
you can do that
through GitHub in a way

00:41:49.420 --> 00:41:52.200
that you're already used to.

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:55.000
And we can also attach
different types of CPUs

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:59.060
or attach GPUs to those
platform notebooks as well.

00:41:59.060 --> 00:42:01.270
So if you've got tasks where
you're using TensorFlow,

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:03.130
for example, you
can choose which

00:42:03.130 --> 00:42:06.095
GPUs you want that are
appropriate for that.

00:42:06.095 --> 00:42:08.470
And then, of course, you can
always host your own Jupyter

00:42:08.470 --> 00:42:09.650
notebook as well.

00:42:09.650 --> 00:42:11.950
So if you already have your
own environment set up,

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:13.980
and you've already
got hosted notebooks,

00:42:13.980 --> 00:42:16.230
bring into BigQuery
Client Library,

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:18.910
and you can now utilize
the benefits of BigQuery

00:42:18.910 --> 00:42:21.010
with the notebooks that
you've already got.

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:22.870
So let's take a look
at a couple other tools

00:42:22.870 --> 00:42:25.930
we have here as well
for doing analysis,

00:42:25.930 --> 00:42:28.612
and we're going to look at
Dataproc being one of those.

00:42:28.612 --> 00:42:30.820
And then we'll get into a
couple of the partner tools

00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:31.695
as well that we have.

00:42:34.300 --> 00:42:37.005
So first off, one of the partner
tools that I want to mention,

00:42:37.005 --> 00:42:38.940
a recent addition
here, is that we now

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:42.000
have support for Parabricks
through the Marketplace

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:43.950
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:42:43.950 --> 00:42:47.190
So Parabricks has written
a GPU-optimized version

00:42:47.190 --> 00:42:51.600
of GATK Best Practices and
Deep Variant, and in this case,

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:54.570
they're able to take what
was about a 30-hour run

00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:57.970
and turn that into 45 minutes.

00:42:57.970 --> 00:43:01.380
So this is now available for you
to launch in Cloud Marketplace.

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:04.350
It runs in your
environment and allows

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:10.080
you to bring services like this
to run in your own project.

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:11.940
The other part to
note is if you're

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:14.802
a developer who's
building tools like this,

00:43:14.802 --> 00:43:16.260
this is a way that
you can actually

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:19.120
provide that and make
that available as well

00:43:19.120 --> 00:43:22.380
and simplify running that on
Google Cloud's infrastructure

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:26.920
so that your end customers don't
have to figure any of that out.

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:28.890
So let's take a look
at going through here.

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:31.830
We'll start by pulling
it up in Marketplace.

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:33.870
Go ahead and launch
that on compute,

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:37.410
and we can choose the types
of GPUs we want, like a V100.

00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:39.720
We can choose how many
we want to attach,

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:42.090
and go ahead and start that.

00:43:42.090 --> 00:43:45.870
Once it's up and running, we're
going to SSH into our instance.

00:43:45.870 --> 00:43:49.440
And in this case, we're going
to copy our license over

00:43:49.440 --> 00:43:53.560
and set a couple of inputs
here and a reference as well.

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:57.510
And those are all
coming from GCS buckets.

00:43:57.510 --> 00:43:59.430
We're then going to
go ahead and run that

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:01.780
through this connection.

00:44:01.780 --> 00:44:03.720
And as this is
running, again, it's

00:44:03.720 --> 00:44:06.600
going to process it, save our
output to its storage bucket

00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:08.320
that we defined.

00:44:08.320 --> 00:44:10.140
And again, this
shows it in a way

00:44:10.140 --> 00:44:13.530
that you can now bring this
directly into your project,

00:44:13.530 --> 00:44:15.370
or for the developers
of this tool,

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:17.920
this is a way that they can
deploy and monetize that

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:18.420
as well.

00:44:21.860 --> 00:44:26.390
So lastly, I want to talk about
Dataproc at a high level here.

00:44:26.390 --> 00:44:29.480
So Dataproc is our managed
service for Apache Spark

00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:31.910
and Hadoop environments,
and this can

00:44:31.910 --> 00:44:34.250
be used for a variety of tools.

00:44:34.250 --> 00:44:36.770
One of them right now that
we're using is for Hale,

00:44:36.770 --> 00:44:40.130
and so using this to run a
genomic analysis framework

00:44:40.130 --> 00:44:42.530
doing things like
GWAS, and we're

00:44:42.530 --> 00:44:46.880
able to run that through
Dataproc, which will handle

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:48.810
all of the nodes for you.

00:44:48.810 --> 00:44:50.810
And again, as we look at
the world of notebooks,

00:44:50.810 --> 00:44:53.670
we can also then connect the
notebook to that as well.

00:44:53.670 --> 00:44:56.833
So if we want to just submit
our job directly to that cluster

00:44:56.833 --> 00:44:59.000
or if we want to connect a
notebook to that cluster,

00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:01.242
we can then run the job
through the notebook as well.

00:45:04.550 --> 00:45:06.860
So I hope that gives a
good introduction to some

00:45:06.860 --> 00:45:10.220
of the tools we have in
this space and helps cover

00:45:10.220 --> 00:45:13.750
some of the questions
you might have.

00:45:13.750 --> 00:45:16.510
Some of the resources
that we discussed here

00:45:16.510 --> 00:45:20.450
that are available
with links as well.

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:23.440
And we've got links here to
some of the documentation

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:25.395
and the tutorials that we used.

00:45:25.395 --> 00:45:26.770
And then, as I
mentioned as well,

00:45:26.770 --> 00:45:28.750
we have a mailing
list at the top.

00:45:28.750 --> 00:45:31.330
And that Google Genomics
Discuss mailing list,

00:45:31.330 --> 00:45:34.940
you can join that from our
genomics documentation page.

00:45:34.940 --> 00:45:36.910
And this list
consists of a number

00:45:36.910 --> 00:45:40.180
of users of these products from
around the world as well as

00:45:40.180 --> 00:45:43.240
members of our Solutions team
and our Product and Engineering

00:45:43.240 --> 00:45:44.480
teams.

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:46.450
So if you have issues,
if you have questions,

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:49.360
if something's not working,
if you're trying something new

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:52.210
that you can't figure out, it's
a great place to go and see

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:54.340
if there's someone who may
have already done that,

00:45:54.340 --> 00:45:57.435
if there's other contributors
in the field who can help out,

00:45:57.435 --> 00:45:59.560
or if you've got questions
for the engineering team

00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:02.080
as to how we can help better
improve these products.

00:46:02.080 --> 00:46:05.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

